ucting Empirical  research against a backdrop of delighted  screams and chainsaws and the groans of  zombies a according to matthias's  research when we engage in recreational  fear by walking through a haunted house  or playing a horror game we're buying  into a kind of make-believe world full  of cues that activate biological  responses evolved over millions of years  that's why when you're playing a horror  game your palms get sweaty your heart  beats faster and jump scares make you  leap from your chair  you're putting your body into a state of  fight or flight and it's reacting as  though those cues are coming from real  dangers but and this is the key part  because we know it's not real we can  instead enjoy the rush of Sensations we  get from activating those biological  responses and practice our coping  strategies for scary situations as a  kind of play what Matthias and his team  have found through their research is  that there is a kind of inverted  u-shaped relationship between enjoyment  and fear levels if the the fear level is  too low it's a weak boring but if it's  too scary it's not enjoyable either this  means that there's a sweet spot for  every individual the just right amount  of fear in order for them to have a good  time this relationship is a lot like the  one we see with the feeling of flow  check out our previous episode of the  show all about that and much like flow  video games offer the player a great  opportunity to balance their levels of  fear and enjoyment I can't influence the  the way the story unfolds when I'm  watching a horror movie or reading a  book but when I'm playing amnesia the  Dark Descent I can decide not to go into  the room from which a weird noise comes  and so that feeling of agency that  feeling of being able to influence what  happens leads to a higher degree of  presence of investment and because it's  pretend because there is no real risk no  real danger we can reap the benefits we  can get all the pleasure out 